  {
    "id": "kangaroo-ca-2012-g1112-17",
    "bank": "kangaroo",
    "source": {
      "contest": "Canadian Math Kangaroo",
      "paper": "Grade 11-12",
      "year": 2012,
      "number": 17,
      "url": "https://api.mathkangaroo.ca/api/v1/media/2012/2012gr1112e.pdf"
    },
    "grade": "11-12",
    "points": 4,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Circles $C_1$ and $C_2$ are centred at $O_1$ and $O_2$, respectively. $AB$ is a diameter of $C_1$; $C$ is a point on $C_1$; $C_2$ is tangent to the lines $AC$ and $BC$, and $\\angle BAC = x$. What is the measure of the angle $\\angle O_2CO_1$?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2012-g1112/q17.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$180^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$180^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$135^\\circ + \\alpha$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$135^\\circ - \\alpha$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "It depends on the radii of the circles"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Angle chase",
        "body": "Write $\\alpha$ for $x = \\angle BAC$.\n\n- $AB$ is a diameter, so $\\angle ACB = 90^\\circ$ (Thales).\n- $O_1A = O_1C$, so $\\angle O_1CA = \\angle O_1AC = \\alpha$ and $\\angle O_1CB = 90^\\circ - \\alpha$.\n- $C_2$ lies in the angle vertically opposite to $\\angle ACB$ (between the extensions of $AC$ and $BC$ beyond $C$). Its centre $O_2$ lies on the bisector of that $90^\\circ$ angle, so $CO_2$ makes $45^\\circ$ with the ray $CB'$ that extends $BC$ beyond $C$.\n- The ray $CB'$ makes $180^\\circ - \\angle O_1CB = 90^\\circ + \\alpha$ with $CO_1$.\n\n$CO_2$ and $CO_1$ are on opposite sides of the ray $CB'$, so $\\angle O_2CO_1 = 45^\\circ + 90^\\circ + \\alpha = 135^\\circ + \\alpha$.\n\n**Answer: (C)**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Check with coordinates",
        "body": "Take $C_1$ the unit circle, $A = (-1,0)$, $B = (1,0)$ and $\\alpha = 30^\\circ$, so $C = (\\cos 60^\\circ, \\sin 60^\\circ) = (\\tfrac12, \\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2})$. The unit vectors from $C$ away from $A$ and away from $B$ are $(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}, \\tfrac12)$ and $(-\\tfrac12, \\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2})$; their sum points at $75^\\circ$ — the direction of $CO_2$. The direction of $CO_1$ is $240^\\circ$. The angle between them is $240^\\circ - 75^\\circ = 165^\\circ = 135^\\circ + 30^\\circ$. Only (C) fits.\n\n**Answer: (C)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
